### SCENARIO 1 — a full lifecycle, and it ends by asserting the STATE OF THE WORLD.
###
### An application does not make one request. It creates a parent, hangs children off it, narrows and
### orders them, reads one, changes it under its own version, removes another — and what matters at
### the end is what is left. Every step below consumes what an earlier step returned, and the closing
### assertion is the exact contents of the collection, field by field, not "the last call was 200".
###
### This scenario owns its own customer, so the set it asserts is complete rather than "the seeded
### seven plus whatever this file added".

### 1. The parent.
# @name CreateCustomer
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "name": "Lifecycle Ltd",
  "tier": "standard",
  "email": "ops@lifecycle.test"
}

### 2. Four children, all referencing the parent by the id step 1 returned.
# @name CreateChildA
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8001",
  "title": "Survey the site",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "quoted_price": 200.00,
  "access_code": "LC-8001",
  "customer_id": "{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

###
# @name CreateChildB
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8002",
  "title": "Install the unit",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 2,
  "quoted_price": 4200.00,
  "access_code": "LC-8002",
  "customer_id": "{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

###
# @name CreateChildC
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8003",
  "title": "Commission the unit",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 3,
  "quoted_price": 600.00,
  "access_code": "LC-8003",
  "customer_id": "{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The fourth is the one that gets cancelled and deleted, so it starts out different
# @name CreateChildD
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8004",
  "title": "Remove the old unit",
  "status": "cancelled",
  "priority": 4,
  "quoted_price": 150.00,
  "access_code": "LC-8004",
  "customer_id": "{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### 3. List the children, filtered to this customer and to the live ones, in dispatch order.
# @name ListScheduledChildren
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?customer_id=eq.{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}&status=eq.scheduled&order=priority.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### 4. Read one of them, and take its version.
# @name ReadChildB
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateChildB.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### 5. Change it under the version the read handed out.
# @name UpdateChildB
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateChildB.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
If-Match: {{ReadChildB.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "in_progress",
  "assigned_to": "{{userTechNorth}}",
  "quoted_price": 4550.00
}

### 6. Remove the cancelled one.
# @name DeleteChildD
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateChildD.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### 7. THE STATE OF THE WORLD. Everything this customer now has, in a known order.
# @name FinalCollection
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?customer_id=eq.{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}&order=reference.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### The deleted one is gone, by id, not merely absent from a page
# @name DeletedChildIsGone
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateChildD.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### The updated one carries the new values, read back rather than trusted from the write's echo
# @name ReadUpdatedChild
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateChildB.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### An untouched sibling is byte-for-byte what it was created as
# @name ReadUntouchedChild
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateChildA.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### And the parent still cannot be deleted while a child references it (`onDelete: restrict`) — refused as
### the caller's conflict it is (#138), not as an internal error
# @name DeleteParentWithChildren
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [409]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers/{{CreateCustomer.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
